Answer: Declare the five properties as private. Provide public setters that validate the values before assigning them, public getters that return the values, and a public study() method. The application class creates a Student object, updates it through setters, reads it through getters, and calls study(). Java Code:

```java
class Student {    private int id;    private String name;    private int age;    private int marks;    private String college;
public void setId(int id) {        if (id > 0) {            this.id = id;        } else {            System.out.println("Invalid ID");        }    }
public int getId() {        return id;    }
public void setName(String name) {        if (name != null && name.length() >= 3) {            this.name = name;        } else {            System.out.println("Invalid name");        }    }
public String getName() {        return name;    }
public void setAge(int age) {        if (age > 18) {            this.age = age;        } else {            System.out.println("Invalid age");        }    }
public int getAge() {        return age;    }
public void setMarks(int marks) {        if (marks > 20) {            this.marks = marks;        } else {            System.out.println("Invalid marks");        }    }
public int getMarks() {        return marks;    }
public void setCollege(String college) {        this.college = college;    }
public String getCollege() {        return college;    }
public void study() {        System.out.println("Student is studying");    }}
public class StudentApp {    public static void main(String[] args) {        Student student = new Student();
student.setId(10);        student.setName("Rommel");        student.setAge(25);        student.setMarks(90);        student.setCollege("StringStack Institute of Technology");
System.out.println(student.getId());        System.out.println(student.getName());        System.out.println(student.getAge());        System.out.println(student.getMarks());        System.out.println(student.getCollege());        student.study();    }}
```

Sample Input: No input Sample Output: 10Rommel2590StringStack Institute of TechnologyStudent is studying

Answer: Declare the product properties as private, initialize them through a constructor that accepts the product details, and provide a getter for each property. The ProductApp class creates the object by passing arguments to new Product(...) and reads the details through the getters. The values below are illustrative examples of the constructor call. Java Code:

```java
class Product {    private int id;    private String name;    private double price;    private int stock;    private String description;
public Product(int id, String name, double price, int stock, String description) {        this.id = id;        this.name = name;        this.price = price;        this.stock = stock;        this.description = description;    }
public int getId() {        return id;    }
public String getName() {        return name;    }
public double getPrice() {        return price;    }
public int getStock() {        return stock;    }
public String getDescription() {        return description;    }}
public class ProductApp {    public static void main(String[] args) {        Product product = new Product(            10, "Laptop", 2000.0, 5, "Portable computer"        );
System.out.println(product.getId());        System.out.println(product.getName());        System.out.println(product.getPrice());        System.out.println(product.getStock());        System.out.println(product.getDescription());    }}
```

Sample Input: No input Sample Output: 10Laptop2000.05Portable computer

Answer: Create the object with new and the class name followed by empty parentheses. If the class has no declared constructor, the compiler provides a default no-argument constructor, so the object can be created as shown below. Java Code:

```java
class Car {    // No constructor is declared in this class.}
public class DefaultConstructorDemo {    public static void main(String[] args) {        Car car = new Car();        System.out.println("Car object created");    }}
```

Sample Input: No input Sample Output: Car object created
